% ============================================================================
% ocean-current-surface-position — wind-driven currents → where in the
% ocean they occur, decoded from a span already sitting unused inside the
% SAME NOAA source `ocean-current-drivers.adj` already cites
% (adj-facts-stdlib, OCEANOGRAPHY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `ocean-current-drivers.adj`
% (`ocean_current_driver(current_type, driver)`, ONE physical driver per
% current type — wind_driven_currents → wind): a new
% `ocean_current_surface_position(current_type, position)` table names
% WHERE the SAME already-cited NOAA "What is a current?" page states
% wind-driven currents occur, decoded from a clause already sitting
% unused inside that table's own header truth table -- no new WebFetch.
% That table's schema has room for only the DRIVER; the already-quoted
% wind-driven-currents sentence also carries a positional fact that never
% made it into a row:
%
%   wind_driven_currents → at_or_near_the_oceans_surface
%     "Winds drive currents that are at or near the ocean's surface."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? ocean_current_surface_position(wind_driven_currents, $Position)  % at_or_near_the_oceans_surface
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like the
% parent's own): the single `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `ocean_current_surface_position(current_type, position)` carrying the
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query -- the
% engine returns the position WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on any current type that is not the one
% tabled here (it never invents a position). The query also runs
% BACKWARD -- bind a position and recall which current type occurs there:
%
%     ? ocean_current_surface_position($CurrentType, at_or_near_the_oceans_surface)  % wind_driven_currents
%
% This is honestly a ONE-ROW table, not a forced three-row one: neither
% the tidal_currents nor the thermohaline_circulation quote already
% sitting inside the parent table's own header names a comparably clean
% SURFACE-vs-DEEP position for that current type -- tidal_currents'
% cited span names a shore-proximity/strength fact (a DIFFERENT kind of
% positional claim, requiring its own atomization judgment call, tracked
% separately), and thermohaline_circulation's cited span
% ("...density differences in water due to temperature (thermo) and
% salinity (haline) variations in different parts of the ocean.") names
% no position at all -- so this table correctly abstains on both rather
% than inventing one.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME wind-driven-currents
% span already quoted inside `ocean-current-drivers.adj`'s own
% header/provenance section, from the SAME National Oceanic and
% Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) "What is a current?" page that table
% already cites -- no new WebFetch, only a different schema decoding a
% part of that already-verified quote. `trust authoritative` -- the same
% tier the parent table already carries for this row (a primary U.S.
% government .gov source).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table ocean_current_surface_position {
    columns current_type, position

    row (wind_driven_currents, at_or_near_the_oceans_surface)

    source "Winds drive currents that are at or near the ocean's surface."
    locator "https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/current.html"
    trust authoritative
}
